            {
              "text": "Examples",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Dark plays Start Your Engines! at the start of the game, which instructs Dark to deploy some podracer-related cards \"...and any Effect that deploys for free. \" Dark must deploy a card with exactly \" Effect \" in the subtype box-not a Starting Effect, Political Effect, or Immediate Effect.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 82,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Later in the game, Dark plays Twi'lek Advisor (\"Use 3 Force to search your Reserve Deck and take one Eff ect of any kind into hand; reshuffle\"). Dark may take any Effect, including those mentioned above, because it says \"of any kind.\" He could take into hand a Starting Effect (though he couldn't deploy it because Starting Effects only deploy at the start of game). He takes into hand Frozen Dinner, an Immediate Effect. Because of this-and because its game text does not indicate it",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "must respond to another action-it can be deployed at any time.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Epic Events",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A card type which represents a major storyline occurrence (the destruction of the Death Star, the turning of Luke Skywalker etc.). Epic Events are unique in that they are the only card type which can either play or deploy, depending on their game text: At tack Run Used Pile, and in this way the chance often arises to and Epic Duel deploy on table (like Effects) and thus may be utilized more than once, whereas Commence Primary Ignition and Target The Main Generators play (like Interrupts) and are placed in the Used Pile or Lost Pile after use, according to thei rresult.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Interrupts",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Type of card representing a sudden short term change (such as the surprise attack by the Ewok army or Luke blinding Vader with carbonite gas). Interrupts may be played during any player's turn, and once it has its effect is then dis carded. An Interrupt may play at any time between other actions, or as a response to an action that the Interrupt specifically cancels or modifies.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The exact conditions required are listed in the interrupt's game text. If no condition is stated, then the i nterrupt is a legal card play whenever a \"top level\" action is possible for that player (i.e. no other actions are resolving). To play one, say, \"Interrupt!\" and bring out the card. Interrupt cards play, have their result and thengo to the Used Pile or the Lost Pile; they are not considered to be \"on table\" while they are executing.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Interrupts play, they do not deploy. If an Interrupt instructs you to place it somewhere other than where that subtype of interrupt would normally be placed after resolving (forexample, a Used Interrupt that tells you to place it back in your hand), follow the instruction on the interrupt (unless canceled).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The following subtypes or Interrupts exist, and may even exist on the same interrupt card (this will be noted in their gametext and their description box): Used, Lost, and Starting. If an interrupt has multiple functions, the owner playing it chooses which function is being used at the time the interrupt is played.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Some cards cause Interrupts to change subtype (e.g.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Wookiee causes the Lost Interrupt Wookiee Strangle to be a Used Interrupt instead). If there is a conflict where an Interrupt is being changed to a Used Interrupt and also being changed to a Lost Interrupt, then it is a Lost Interrupt.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "An Interrupt's subtype is determined during initiation and will not change after that, unless specifically noted otherwise (e.g. Voyeur). For example, in the unlikely event that Wookiee leaves table after the initiation of Wookiee Strangle, it remains a Used Interrupt.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Interr upts",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Lost",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Lost Interrupts are identified by their interrupt icon in the upper-left corner of their card, and \"Lost Interrupt\" in their subtype box. Lost interrupts (or interrupts with a Lost function) are played under standard interrupt rules.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Once resol ved they are placed in their owner's Lost Pile.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Interrupts",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Used",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Used Interrupts are identified by their interrupt icon in the upper-left corner of their card, and \"Used Interrupt\" in their subtype box. Used interrupts (or interrupts with a Used function) are played under standard interrupt rules. Once resolved they are placed in their owner's play them more than once. It should be noted that if canceled, a Used interrupt is still placed in its owner's Lost Pile, not the Used pile.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Interrupts",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Starting",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Starting Interrupts are identified by their interrupt icon in the upper-left corner of their card, and \"Starting Interrupt\" in their subtype box. Starting interrupts (or interrupts with a Starting func tion) are played after both players' starting locations (or objective and objective starting cards) have been deployed, and before Reserve Decks have been shuffled to draw opening hands. Each player may play one starting Interrupt. See Starting the game, Ch. 2. Both players reveal their starting interrupt simultaneously. If both players choose to use a starting interrupt, the player who goes first in the game must resolve his starting interrupt first, then the opponent must resolve his starting interrupt. The player going second thus has an opportunity to view the cards his opponent has chosen before searching for her own. Where the Starting interrupt is placed after it resolves is stated on the actual interrupt card. A 'Starting' function on an Interrupt may never be altered in a way so that it might play as any other subtype of Interrupt (Used, Lost, etc). In addition, an Interrupt performing a 'Starting' function may not be targeted to be 'grabbed.'",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Jedi Tests",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A card type that represents the ability to learn the ways of the Force. Completing Jedi Tests gives you new capabilities that can have far-reaching consequences, and can also raise your character's ability.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "To learn how to use Jedi Tests, see Jedi Testing, Ap. C.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Locations",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Types of cards that repres ent the many exotic places shown in the Star Wars universe. Locations are where Force is generated and where characters, starships, vehicles etc., are deployed. All location cards have a destiny value of zero. Location cards are placed on table in a horizo ntal line between the two players.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 83,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Related locations are played next to each other to form a group. Locations belonging to different systems are separated by a gap. The cards are oriented so that, when deployed, the Light Side player is facing the side wit hthe blue lightsaber icons and the Dark Side player is facing the side with the red lightsaber icons. Game text on one side of a location should be considered a 'global effect' (it affects both players) unless context clearly specifies otherwise (usually by use of words like 'you,' 'your,' or 'opponent's') or if the text is somehow canceled. Note that some locations may contain",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "duplicate text on both sides of the Force, in such cases the redundant text should be ignored (for example, modifiers may not be double-counted). There are two notable exceptions to this rule:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Text that provides a Force drain modifier only affects the player on the corresponding side of the Force.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Text that allows a player to initiate an optional action (or causes him to initiate a mandatory action) only affects the player on the corresponding side of the Force, unless context specifies otherwise.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Locations have no deploy cost. You may deploy any number of location cards from your hand, for free, during any of your deploy phases. There are three kinds",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Exterior Sites",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "of location: site, system and sector.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Site locations represent different areas on a planet (or within the Death Star or Cloud City) where characters and vehicles may deploy, battle and move. Site locations related to a system may be deployed regardless of whether that system card is already on table (and vice versa).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "System locations represent different planets (or Death Stars) in the galaxy. They are deployed on the end of a row of 'related' site locations (if already in play).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Otherw ise a system may be deployed alone. A system card is related to all sites in the same system (e.g., the Tatooine system is related to all Tatooine sites).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Sector locations represent different altitudes of airspace over a planet (for cloud sectors) or areas within an asteroid belt (for asteroid sectors). They are deployed on either side of a system location, and thus the system location card must already be on table or you to deploy sectors in that system. Bespin: Cloud City, is the only sector that may deploy without the system on table.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Converting Locations",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Many locations have both a Light Side and a Dark Side version. (Be aware that the game text and Force icons will vary!) Only one of each unique (•) location may be in play at a time. A location in play can be converted by deploying the opposing side's version on top (you may not convert your own locations). It is possible for a location to change back and forth (Light to Dark, Dark to Light) several times during a game. Any action that permits you to deploy a location may be used to convert an opponent's location; in fact, if you attempt to deploy a location from a deck or pile, and the only legal location would convert your opponent's location, you must deploy it.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "After a location has been converted, only the previous relationship begins after the site is deployed; thus, for version(s) (found underneath the newest version) are referred to as \"converted locations\". These converted locations are in a supporting state and may be targeted only by actions that explicitly target converted locations.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Some actions, such as on Underworld Contacts, state that they raise a converted location to the top. Raising a converted location to the top is considered to be a special act of converting locations: unless specified otherwise, you may raise either side's location to the end of a system's exterior locations, separating them top, you may do so at any time (not only in the deploy phase), and you may do so regardless of whose version was previously on top. If a location has been converted multiple times, any of the previous versions can be raised to the top (again, provided they meet the specified requirements, if any were listed).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Deploying/Arranging Locations",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Locations should be arranged and deployed as laid out in Appendix E.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Locations",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Sites",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Horizontally oriented location where characters and",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "other cards can dep loy, battle and move.",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "There are planet, mobile, vehicle, and starship sites (as indicated by their icons).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "A site location which is conceptually 'outside' (or partially outside); identified by an exterior icon.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Interior Sites A site lo",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "cation which is conceptually 'inside' (or partially",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "inside), identified by an interior icon.",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "At sites that are interior-only (i.e., those without an exterior icon),",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• characters and vehicles may not shuttle; and",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• vehicles may not deploy or move unless speci fically allowed to on the card.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "For example, a vehicle may move to Docking Bay 94 occupants want to gointo the Cantina they must disembark and move inside, leaving their vehicle at a nearby exterior site.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "GenericS ites",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Generic sites are a special type of site location having one or more  symbols next to its title. (See Unique, Restricted, & Non -Unique, Ch. 9)",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "These locations represent environments found on a variety of different planets. They may be deployed only as part of a planet system that is already represented on table by a system location or a non-generic site.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, note the following restrictions:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Unless specifically stated otherwise, generic sites may not deploy to Ahch -To, Bespin, or Hoth due to the special circumstances of these planets' environments and layouts.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Many generic sites have additional restrictions listed in their game text.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Once deployed to a planet system, a generic site takes on the name of that system and is thus related to the system, its other sites and its cloud sectors. (This example, the game text of the Dagobah system does not allow you to take a generic Swamp from your Reserve Deck.) Your opponent may convert your generic site on table with another generic site of the same name (excluding the name of the planet it has taken on). Most generic sites are exterior-only, and thus are grouped with the other exterior sites on the planet.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 84,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Interior/exterior gen eric sites should be placed at the from any interior sites (including other interior/exterior locations).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "See Blown Away, Ap. Cand Jedi Testing, Ap. Cfor the interaction of generic sites with those events. is aboard a capital starship, the",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Executor",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": ", and a Super -",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Holosites",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The Dejarik Hologameboard and Imperial Holotable are both holosites, with their own special rules. See Dejarik Rules, Ap. C.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Marker Sites",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Any exterior Hoth site (except Echo Docking Bay) and the Wampa Cave. The lowest numbered site is the \"innermost\" and the highest is the \"outermost.\"",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "\"May Not Be Separated From X\"",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Sometimes a site's game text will state that it \"may not be separated\" from another specific named site. When such a site is on table, the specific named site can only be deploye dadjacent to it. Likewise, if the specific named site is on table, the site with the \"May not be separated\" text can only be deployed adjacent to it.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "When two sites that \"may not be separated\" from each other are both on table, no sites may be deployed be tween them (even if the two sites that \"may not be separated\" are somehow not adjacent to each other).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Actions that rearrange sites (e.g. Retract The Bridge) may be played, but the final result must ensure that any sites that \"may not be separated\" from eachother are adjacent to each other.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "As a special rule, \"May not be separated\" text is unaffected by Expand The Empire, and can never be canceled (even if the site is collapsed or blown away).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starship Sites and Vehicle Sites",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Starship and vehicle sites obey location deployment rules (e.g., interior/exterior sequence and insertion) and create locations aboard a starship/vehicle where cards may deploy, battle and move. For convenience, each group of starship sites is placed on table separately from other sites. However, the group is related to one particular starship.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "There are two types of starship/vehicle sites: unique, and non-unique. Non-unique sites can be related to any card that has an aspect mentioned in its title (for instance, Star Destroyer: Launch Bay may be related to anything considered a star destroyer). Non-unique sites deploy only to a starship/vehicle on table, and are lost if that starship/vehicle leaves table.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Unique starship/vehicle sites are instead related to one specific starship/vehicle in their name (such as",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Executor",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": ": Control Room, it is related only to the",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Executor",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "). They operate independently of their related starship/vehicle, which means they can deploy whether or not the starship/vehicle is on table, and do not leave play even if the related starship/vehicle does (however, see Blown Away, Ap. Cfor dealing with blown away starships).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Cards at a starship/vehicle site are considered to be on (and aboard) a starship or vehicle, and they are considered on or aboard a sta rship/vehicle mentioned in the site's title. Also, they are considered to be on (and aboard) the related vehicle and starship (if it is on table). For instance, if Stormtrooper is at Star",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Destroyer: Launch Bay related to Flagship",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Executor",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": ", he",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Class Star Destroyer. A Rebel Trooper at",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Home One",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": ":",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "War Room is aboard a starship and",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Home One",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": ".",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, if the Rebel Trooper is at the",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Home One",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": ": War Room while",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Home One",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "is not on table, then that is all he is aboard (he is aboard",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Home One",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "and aboard a starship, but not aboard a capital starship or a cruiser, because that information is not on the starship site, so only exists when",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Home One",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "is on table). Cards at starship /vehicle sites are considered to be \"below decks\" rather than on the \"bridge\"/\"cockpit\" of the starship/vehicle, and thus are not considered in any way to be at the location of the vehicle or starship. For instance, if",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Home One",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "and Flagship",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Executor",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "from our above examples were both at Endor, neither the Stormtrooper nor the Rebel Trooper would be considered at Endor or have any effect on any battle that might take place there.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starship/vehicle sites are related to any other starship/vehicle sites related to the same starship/vehicle. While the starship/vehicle is at a site, its sites are considered adjacent (and related) to that site, and are considered related to locations related to that adjacent site. If not at a site, they are not adjacent and are not related to any other location (except other related vehicle/starship sites).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, while such sites may be related, they are not considered sites on that planet or deploying to that planet. For instance, Jabba's Sail Barge: Passenger Deck is not a Tatooine site for the purposes of My Kind Of Scum, and controlling the Sandcrawler: Loading Bay (on a Sandcrawler assuming it is present at a Dantooine site) cannot count towards flipping Dantooine Base Operations.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "For movement related to stars hip/vehicle sites, see Regular -Starship And Vehicle Sites, Ch. 7 and Regular -Landing And Taking Off, Ch. 7).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Locations",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Sectors",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Vertically oriented location where starfighters and other",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "cards can deploy, battle and move.",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "All sectors share the followin gfeatures:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Sectors are oriented vertically, like systems; however, they have no parsec number and thus cannot be used for hyperspeed movement.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Characters may not deploy or move to a sector unless aboard a vehicle or starship which is allowed at that sec tor.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Vehicles may deploy aboard a starship (capacity permitting) that is allowed at a sector.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Certain vehicles are permitted to deploy and move to cloud sectors (see Cloud Sectors), however any vehicle may be carried to any sector if the carrying card is permitted to move to the sector.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 85,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Cards which affect a starship at a system may not necessarily affect a starship at a sector. Refer to the latest game text of the card in question and check if it mentions sectors.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Asteroid sectors and cloud sectors deploy only to planet systems. Most are typically diamond restricted () Number on system locations symbolizing the relative locations (See Unique, Restricted, & Non -Unique, Ch. hyper-route distance between systems. (See Movement 9).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Non-unique sectors (e.g. <><><>Clouds, <><><>Asteroid Field, <>Big One) deploy only to a planet system card that is already on table, by deploying directly next to that planet system or next to another sector of the same type that is already on the table.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Unique sectors (e.g. •Bespin: Cloud City) deploy regardless of whether the related system card is on planet, but are not part of it (e.g., a starship in the table. However, some sectors also have deployment restrictions in their game text (e.g. •Death Star II:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Coolant Shaft).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Sectors may be inserted in any order, as long as you maintain the legal pattern (see Location Deployment, Ap. E).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "At sector locations, you may make any applicable 'reacts' and any applicable unlimited moves. In addition, certain regular moves are allowed at sectors, depending on the kind of sector (see asteroid sectors and cloud sectors).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Asteroid Sectors",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Asteroid sectors are related to a system location, but not to the planet's sites. They are not part of the planet and thus do not take on its name (e.g., asteroid sectors at Dagobah are not \"Dagobah locations\" and thus are not affected by Dagobah's deployment restrictions). If the planet is \"blown away,\" asteroid sectors there are not destroyed.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starships may deploy, battle and move at asteroid sectors.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Cloud Sectors",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Cloud sectors are placed between a system location and its related sites, and represent various \"altitudes\" of air space above the planet's surface. Cloud sectors are related to the planet system where they are deployed and to that planet's sites. They are part of the planet and thus take on its name (i.e., cloud sectors at Tatooine are Tatooine locations, effectively becoming \"Tatooine: Clouds\"). If the planet is \"blown away,\" cloud sectors there are destroyed (lost).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Shuttle vehicles, cloud cars, patrol craft, squadrons, and starfighters (or any starship that moves like a starfighter) may deploy, battle and move at cloud sectors.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Any planet sector with \" cloud \" in its title is considered a cloud sector.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Death Star II Sectors",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Neither player may deploy, battle or initiate Force drains at a Death Star IIsector.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Locations",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Systems",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Vertically oriented location where starships and other cards can deploy, battle and move. Systems use the same card template as sectors but also have a parsec number. There are planet, space and mobile systems.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Parsec Numbers",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-regular-using hyperspeed, Ch. 7.)",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Planet",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The term 'planet' includes a planet system location and all of its sites and cloud sectors. Phrases such as \"at that planet\" and \"on that planet\" have their normal English meanings (see Prepositions, Ch. 4). Asteroid sectors grouped next to a planet are 'related' to the asteroids is not 'at' the planet).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Space",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Sites and sectors cannot be deployed to space systems.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Mobile",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Cloud and asteroid sectors (and generic sites) cannot be deployed to mobile systems. Mobile systems can move; see Movement -Regular -Mobile Systems, Ch. 7.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Locations",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Battleground",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Any system, sector or site location where both players",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "have Force icons.",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "It does not matter if these Force icons are provided by other cards such as Daughter Of Skywalker or Pre sence Of The Force, as long as they are active at that location (not canceled by a Sleen, for example). Remember that the icon on Jedi Masters and Dark Jedi Masters are not Force icons, and therefore do not affect battleground status.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, the following locations are never battlegrounds:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• holosites,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Dagobah locations,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Ahch -To locations,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Coruscant: Galactic Senate,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• shielded Hoth sites,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Death Star IIsystem and Death Star IIsites when non-virtual Endor Shield ' sconditions are met,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Jabba's Palace: Audience Chamber when Bo Shuda is deployed there,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 86,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Tatooine: Podrace Arena while either player has a race total > 0. While Expand The Empire is deployed on the Tatooine: Podrace Arena, the adjacent sites are also prohibi ted from being battlegrounds while either player has a race total > 0.",
