              "text": "Underground",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Podracers",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Type of card that represents one of the renowned machines that Podracer pilots use to compete in the dangerous high-speed sport of Podracing. The mechanics for a Podrace are contained on the Epic Event card Boonta Eve Podrace. Although you can participate in a race without a Podracer card (the Epic Event permits you to place race destiny on the Podrace Arena), you'll find your chances of winning are greatly enhanced when using cards like Sebulba's Podracer or Anakin's Podracer. All references to Podracer target a card of type Podracer. This means that cards such as A Step Backwards cannot be played against race destiny that is stacked on the Podrace Arena.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Podracer cards deploy during your Deploy Phase (for free unless specified otherwise) to your side of the race location indicated in their game text. You do not need presence or Force icons to deploy your Podracer. Your podracer is considered to be present, and \"at\" that location.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "For the rules of podracing, see Podracing, Ap. C.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starships",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Type of card representing the many machines used to travel and battle throughout the galaxy in space.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starships are divided into three distinct subtypes-capital starships, starfighters and squadrons.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starships also come in different types based on the presence (or absence) or certain icons.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starships",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "capital",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Capital starships represent the larger fleet style starships of the Star Wars galaxy, such as Imperial Star Destroyers or Mon Calamari Cruisers. Capital starships are identified by their starship icon in the upper-left characters, weapons and devices to the squadron if corner of their card, and the keyword \"Capital\" in their description box (just below the picture).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starships",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "starfighter",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Starfighters represent the smaller class of starships, often manned by only a single pilot, or a small number of crew. Examp les include the TIE Fighter and the Alliance's X-wing Fighter. Starfighters are identified by their starship icon in the upper-left corner of their card, and the keyword \"Starfighter\" in their description box (just below the picture).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starships",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "squadron",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A squadron is a class of starship card, separate from starfighter and capital starship, that represents more than one starship (usually of the same class) on one card. Squadron class starships are identified by their starship icon in the upper-left corner of their card, and the keyword \"Squadron\" in their description box (just below the picture). It should be noted that not all starships with Squadron in their title are squadron class starships. Some titles, such as Gold Squadron 1, simply represents their flight squadron designations, and does not make them a squadron class starship (see squadron designations). A squadron is treated as:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• one card",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• one starship",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• multiples of its starship class(es).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "For example, a B -Wing Squadron is considered to be 3 B -Wings, while the Death Star Assault Squadron counts as three TIEs (one TIE Advanced x1, 2 TIE/lns). A squadron is NOT targeted or treated as a starfighter in any way, with ONE exception. The components of squadrons may be treated as starfighters ONLY for capacity purposes aboard starships that can carry starfighters. Any game action that takes or places a squadron component (X-wings, Y-wings, TIEs etc.) to or from a deck or pile (retrieve, deploy from Reserve, place in Used Pile etc.) can only work with a squadron if it affects all three of the starfighters depicted on the squadron card. Similarly, an action that moves a component of a squadron (such as You're All Clear Kid, which moves a TIE) can only move the squadron if it can move all of its components. The only exception to this rule is that any game action that causes one of the squadron's components to be lost, captured, or placed out of play affects the entire card.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 88,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "If a squadron does not have at least as many pilots as it does starships, it is considered unpiloted (see Unpiloted).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
