        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 165,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Dejarik Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "'Dejariks' are small holographic gamepieces representing lifeforms from various planets in the galaxy. Any Interrupt or Effect with \"dejarik\" or \"hologram\" in lore is a dejarik or hologram, respectively hologram). Dejariks and holograms may battle each other at a holosite (Dejarik Hologameboard or Imperial Holotable). A holosite is placed by itself on table and is allow dejariks and holograms to deploy and battle there (or deploying to) a holosite are not considered to be Interrupts and Effects (except for cards that specifically holograms. For example, Alter may not cancel Molator specifically names a dejarik or hologram card may still affect that card (e.g., Grimtaash specifically cancels Molator). Vehicles, characters, starships, Utinni Effects etc. cannot be deployed or moved to a holosite. Only cards specifically permitted to deploy there).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Dejarik and hologram cards must deploy as a dejarik or hologram to a holosite; any deployment text not",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "specifically referencing holosites may not be",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "utilized there",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": ". Dejariks and holograms are not characters, and may not be targeted as such.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 165,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "holosite, use its destiny number as its deploy, forfeit, power and ability numbers. A dejarik deployed to the holotable counts as deploying a card (not a character!) with ability. Thus it will suspend the effects of Scum And Villainy, satisfy the requirements of Ability, Ability,",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 165,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "While at the holosite, the card's normal game text is suspended. Dejariks and holograms must obey uniqueness restrictions regardless of where they are on table. Battles at holosites are similar to battles at other sites (including drawing battle destiny if you have 4 or more ability present). If you control the holosite, you may Force drain there and you may also return any of your dejarik/hologram cards there to your hand at any time between other actions, except during battle.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 165,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "You may not deploy a card to the holosite from any place other than your hand (unless action specifically allows you to deploy a dejarik/hologram).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 165,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Dueling",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 165,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Force users. Lightsabers are not required for duels (conceptu ally the lightsaber represents finely-honed skill rather than the physical object). Once a duel has been initiated follow this order: 1) Responses to the initiating: actions to cancel the duel (Jedi Escape, Sense) or that are played as a response to the initi ation (Sith Fury) 2) If the duel is not canceled, both players may play any cards that add duel destinies (Focused Attack) or modify duel destinies ('throwing' Darth Vader's Lightsaber). No other actions are permitted. The player initiating the duel performs the first action.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "3) Follow duel cards directions (usually drawing destiny) to determine winner. No other actions may be performed at this time (except responses to destiny draws, e.g. The Ebb Of Battle) 4) Duel has its result, as stated on the duel card.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, if one or both participants leave table before this step, the duel immediately ends with no result. Responses to a character going lost or out of play would normally occur here. 5) Any automatic actions to the duel are triggered 6) Any optional just actions to the duel may be initiated",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Dueling",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Epic Duel",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "An \"epic duel\" is a type of duel that represents one with long-ranging consequences. The duel between Darth Maul and Qui -Gon on Tatooine was obviously not important, while their fateful match on Naboo possibly changed the course of galactic history. There are many cards which initiate an epic duel including the card titled \"Epic Duel.\" These other cards do not require that Epic Duel be on table in order to initiate an epic duel.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Because of this confusion, any reference to \"Epic Duel\" is a reference to the card title, and \"epic duel\" to a duel which is an epic duel (this is a singular, specific exception to the rules; capitalization never matters in",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "any",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "other circumstance). The objective Hunt Down And Destroy The Jedi/Their Fire Has Gone Out Of The Universe will not be placed out of play if you initiate an epic duel without using the Epic Duel card.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Dueling",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Dueling Captives",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "In some instances a card (such as Take Your Father's Place) may allow a character to duel a captive. The captive is considered active until the results of the duel card are completed (step 4 above). After this, no responses that target the captive by name or persona may be initiated (except by the duel card). Thus in a Vader/Luke duel initiated by Take Your Father's Place, Dark may not dep loy I Am Your Father and Light may not play NOOOOOOOOOOOO!, but the results on the objective will still occur.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Dueling",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Example",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "You Must Confront Vader is on table, targeting Luke Skywalker as the apprentice. You Must Confront Vader says \"Attempt during your move phase when Vader with target (even as a non-frozen captive). Vader and target duel: Each player draws destiny. Add ability. Highest total wins. If target wins, test completed: Leave on table. Add one battle destiny in every battle. Also, target i simmune to attrition < 5.\" During Light's move phase, Luke and Vader are at the same location, so Light initiates the duel. Dark gets first response and plays Lightsaber Parry (\"If opponent just initiated a duel, opponent must choose to lose 4 Force or cancel the duel.\") but Light cancels Lightsaber Parry with Sense. Light gets the next response and passes, then Dark passes. Step 1 is complete and the duel has now begun; it cannot be canceled at this point. During step 2, Light gets first action and passes, then Dark she loses the original Mara's game text permitting her to plays Focused Attack to add a destiny to his total. Both players then pass and Step 3 begins. Light draws his destiny first, and then Dark draws his two destinies.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Appendix C",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Special Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Dark also plays The Ebb Of Battle to add 1 to one of his destiny draws. Dark has the higher total and wins the duel. Nothing happens in Step 4 because You Must Confront Vader doesn't have any special results that occur when Dark wins (it does not specify that Luke is lost, or that Light must lose Force, or anything else). In Step 5, noautomatic actions are triggered. In Step 6, Dark deploys I Am Your Father on Luke as an optional response to Vader having just won a duel.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The next time that it is Light's move phase, Light decides to try again. Luke and Vader are still at the same locat ion, and so Light initiates the duel. This time, neither player has any actions to play in Step 1 or Step",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "2. In Step 3, Light draws a much higher destiny than Dark, causing Luke to win the duel. During step 4, You Must Confront Vader causes several results to play out: the Jedi Test is completed (raising Luke's ability to 6 and enabling Light to add a battle destiny in every battle), and Luke is made immune to attrition < 5. Light also has I Feel The Conflict on table (\" Each time you win a battle or duel, opponent loses 1 Force (cannot be reduced) and stacks lost card here face down.\") so during Step 5, as an automatic response to losing the duel, Dark must lose 1 Force and stack it there. Neither player has any optional responses for Step 6.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Dueling And Epic Dueling",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Example",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Epic Duel is on table. Vader moves to Obi -Wan's site and Dark plays The Circle Is Now Complete. At this point, Dark must declare if they're initiating a regular duel or an epic duel, as that determines how the card is being played. If it was a regular duel, Dark would then pay 1 Force and follow the Interrupt's game text.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, he wishes to have an epic duel, so Epic Duel's game text is examined. It says Vader must be with the specified target (Obi -Wan, according to our interrupt), a ndthe duel is then carried out using the Epic Event's game text only; aside of stating who is doing the dueling, the Interrupt's game text is now ignored (though it may still be canceled, grabbed, etc.).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "After this is over, Dark moves Mara Jade, The Empe ror's Hand over to Luke's site from an adjacent site. Mara's game text allows her to use Vader's Obsession and Epic Duel as if Vader, so Dark may choose to play Vader's Obsession using its game text or Epic Duel's. In this case, the Interrupt's game text is used, and Luke is defeated. The next turn Luke comes back again and deploys directly to Mara's site.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, Dark has another copy of Vader's Obsession.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Because Mara cannot move to his site from an adjacent site (per the wording of Vader's Obsession) she can't have another regular duel, but she can have an epic one. Dark may play the Interrupt using the text of Epic Duel, and the condition of moving over no longer applies.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 166,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "However, fearful that Luke's got something planned, Dark waits, then persona rep laces Mara with her virtual version, which has a permanent lightsaber. However, use those Epic Duel cards.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Hatred Cards",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Some cards, such as The Dark Path (V), may instruct the Dark Side player to stack a card face down as a 'Hatred' card, and/or may target 'Hatred' cards for various gameplay purposes. Because 'Hatred' cards are stacked face down, they are in a Supporting state (see Stacked Cards And Card State, Chapter 1). Any card or action which at tempts to target 'Hatred' cards may target them in their Supporting state.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Also, the Dark Side player may peek at any of their face down Hatred cards at any time. This is a specific exception to the normal rules about viewing face down cards (found in Looking At A Deck, Pile, Or Stack, Chapter 1).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Hoth Energy Shield Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "See Hoth: Main Power Generators, Ap. B.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The Hoth: Main Power Generators site produces an energy shield which is \"strong enough to withstand any bombardment.\" This shield protects much of Hoth for the Light Side by preventing many forms of Dark Side deployment and movement; however, the Dark Side may deploy or land beyond the shield and \"march in\" to Echo Base. When the Main Power Generators site is face up on table, the energy shield covers all Echo Base sites and the first three marker sites. The shieldrd will never extend beyond the 3 marker.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Starship/vehicle sites corresponding to starships or vehicles that are at shielded sites are considered to be shielded sites as well. At shielded sites, the Dark Side may not:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• deploy vehicles, starships or characters (not even spies) unless specifically allowed to deploy to shielded sites;",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• take off, land, shuttle, docking bay transit, perform a Bombing Run, or use abilities that relocate cards (e.g., Elis Helrot, Chief Bast); or",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• add power to battles as a result of starships controlling the system (e.g., from the Hoth system location, a Victory -Class Star Destroyer or Fear Will Keep Them In Line).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The energy shield does not protect the Light Side from a ground assault \"underneath the shield.\" At shielded sites, the Dark Side may",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• move vehicles and characte rsfrom site to site;",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• deploy and use creatures, weapons, Effects, and other cards normally; and",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• perform any actions not otherwise prohibited by these rules.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The energy shield does not restrict the Light Side in any way (because conceptually the Rebels can activate and deactivate the shield to allow their own forces to pass).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "When the energy shield is not active, both sides can deploy and move to Hoth normally.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Shielded Hoth sites are never battlegrounds. See Locations -Battlegrounds, Ch. 9.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Insert Cards",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "An 'insert' card is any card which instructs you to insert it into a Reserve Deck. \"Insert\" is defined as: the act of sliding a card face down into your opponent's Reserve Deck (or face up into your own), then reshuffling. To be",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Appendix C",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Special Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "a valid target for an 'insert' card, a Reserve Deck must contain a minimum of two cards (otherwise there's nothing to insert between). Inserting is a form of deployment, thus any action that allows you to deploy an Effect will allow you to deploy an insert card, and must obeyuniqueness rules.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Insert Cards",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Inserting",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "When inserting a card in your opponent's Reserve Deck, you must insert it face down. (Cards that insert into your own Reserve Deck, such as Access Denied and Restricted Access, specify that they are inserted face up.) The Reserve Deck is shuffled and cut (repeating the shuffle and cut as needed until no 'insert' card is on top of the deck), then replaced.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Insert Cards",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Revealing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "When an 'insert' card reaches the top of the Reserve Deck, it is considered revealed. When an 'insert' is revealed it immediately becomes the topmost action, suspending all further game play. Responses, whether automatic actions (Your Insight Serves You Well) or optional (Don't Forget The Droids) may now be initiated.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "If the 'insert' card is not canceled, it then resolves, then is placed in the Lost Pile. Game play continues.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Insert Cards",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Effects On Gameplay",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Whenever the deck is shuffled, 'insert' cards are shuffled along with it. If at the completion the top card is an 'insert' card, reshuffle until that's no longer the case.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• A Reserve Deck with an 'insert' card in it cannot be counted.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• 'Insert' cards are not considered to be a part of the opponent's Reserve Deck (but instead are considered to be on table). 'Insert' cards can never be activated, lost to satisfy Force damage, used as a destiny draw etc.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Whenever one of your opponent's cards comes to the top of your Reserve Deck, you must reveal it so that both players can see whether it is an 'insert' card. If it is not, return it to thetop of your Reserve Deck, otherwise it is revealed.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• While an opponent's insert is in your Reserve Deck, you must declare to your opponent how much total Force you intend to activate for your normal activation of Force (during your Activate phase), or whe never any action allows you to activate a variable amount of Force. You are then required to activate exactly that amount (although other actions and valid responses may be played during that activation, as normally permitted). If all of the opponent's ins ert cards in your Reserve Deck are revealed during your activation action, after resolving all of them, you have the choice of then activating more than the amount you declared, up to the maximum you are permitted by that activation action.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• If an insert card is revealed during a \"peek\" at your Reserve Deck, it is not considered to have reached the top of your deck yet, and as such does not resolve.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 167,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "In addition, since an insert is not a card in your Reserve Deck, it does not count as a card when peeking, and thus can be ignored. All \"peeked at\"",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "cards that are returned are replaced such that they are on top of the insert card. For example, Dark Side player uses The Dark Path. He peeks at the top two cards, but the third card down in his Reserve Deck has a Light Side back, and when revealed is shown to be Never Tell Me The Odds. He replaces it on the deck, and peeks at the card below it. He then places two of the three cards peeked at in his Lost Pile, and returns one card such that it is on top of the insert card. refers only to Light Side apprentices or only to Dark",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Jedi Testing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The Light Side can train non-droid characters in the use of the Force using Jedi Test cards. Completing Jedi Tests gives you new capabilities that can have far-reaching consequences, and can also raise your character's ability. Attempting Jedi Tests involves bringing an apprentice to a mentor on Dagobah and drawing training destiny (any time the mentor or the apprentice leaves the table, uncompleted Jedi Tests are lost). Each Jedi Test card describes how to begin, attempt and complete the test. The following rules apply to Jedi Tests:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• For every Dagobah site on table (except generic sites), you may add 1 to each training destiny draw.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Each time an apprentice completes a Jedi Test numbered higher than that character's ability, the ability number increases to match the Jedi Test number. For example, completing test #4 raises the apprentice's ability to 4.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• A mentor may train only one apprentice at a time.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• An apprentice may change mentors between Jedi Tests, if necessary.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• An apprentice may notattempt a test that is already placed on that apprentice.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• An apprentice may be targeted by only one uncompleted Jedi Test at a time (targeting is part of the deployment of a Jedi Test, so you cannot deploy a Jedi Test that cannot target an apprentice). Once a character becomes an apprentice, that character's persona remains an apprentice until becoming a Jedi",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• A character may not be an apprentice and a mentor at the same time.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "If a character is replaced by a different version of the same persona, all benefits he or she earned from Jedi training apply to the new version. This is also true if that character converts or crosses over to the other side of the Force. See Crossing Over, Ch. 5.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "If an apprentice (or mentor) becomes inactive (e.g., becomes captured or missing) any completed or uncompleted Jedi Tests targeting that apprentice (or mentor) will \"remember\" such that-when the character is no longer inactive-the apprentice (or mentor) may resume Jedi Training from where he or she left of f. play, but the Light player will not retrieve a Force.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Completed Jedi Tests are deployed on an apprentice, so if the apprentice leaves table their completed Jedi Tests are lost.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Only apprentices may attempt Jedi Tests. If an uncompleted Jedi Test is targeting a character (as the apprentice) that then loses \"apprentice status\" (e.g. replaced with a Jedi version of that persona), then that Jedi Test is lost.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Appendix C",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Special Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Jedi Testing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Apprentice",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "For Light Side, an \"apprentice\" is a non-droid, non -Jedi character who was targeted as such by Great Warrior (Jedi Test #1). Theapprentice must have less ability than its mentor.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "For Dark Side, an \"apprentice\" character is defined by the virtual card Revenge Of The Sith.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Whenever a card references an \"apprentice\" it either",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Sid eapprentices. If that card makes any mention of Jedi Tests or training destiny, then it is referring to Light Side. For example, the card Failure At The Cave mentions Jedi Tests, and the card Reflection mentions training destiny, therefore these cards onl yrefer to Light Side apprentices.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Jedi Testing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Mentor",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A character of ability > 2 who is targeted to train an apprentice.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Jedi Testing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Mind What You Have Learned",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "This objective has numerous effects on the way Jedi Testing works. These changes supersed ethe normal Jedi Testing rules.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Even though the apprentice's Jedi Tests are deployed onto the table, the tests should be treated as if they were still deployed on him or her. Thus, if the apprentice leaves table, they would be lost, if he/she becomes inac tive, so will they, etc. You still obviously follow the text on the Side 7 of the objective that states that while it's up the tests are suspended rather than lost. Completed Jedi Tests cannot be taken into hand from the table.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Mind What You Have Learned",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Deployment Restrictions",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The Side 0 text on this card permits you to ignore Dagobah deployment rules and location deployment restrictions listed in the game text of any version of the Luke or Yoda persona when deploying them to Dagobah using the objective. It also permits you to deploy At Peace during your deploy phase (overriding its printed requirement to deploy at the beginning of your turn.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The Side 7 text does not permit Luke to deploy without presence or Force icons; this is a specific exception to thelocation deployment restrictions rule.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Mind What You Have Learned",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Retrieval",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "An action whose result may be retrieval using cards on Dagobah can still be initiated, but if the result is a retrieval action, no retrieval occurs. For example, the Light Side may still initiate a battle on Dagobah against the Dark Side player even though Draw Their Fire is in",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Similarly, Failure At The Cave will work normally, except that if the destiny draw is < 4, the Dark Side does not retrieve 2 Force.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 168,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Lightsaber Combat",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Lightsaber combat depicts the type of extended battles where a lightsaber-wielding Jedi attempts to wear down his foe through a series of thrusts and parries. Only the strongest of attacks will be enough to defeat your",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "opponent. Lightsaber combat is not a \"duel,\" and as such is not affected by any cards or rules that affect duels (and vice-versa). The objective cards We'll Handle This / Duel Of The Fates and Let Them Make The First Move / At Last We Will Have Revenge detail the conditions, targets, resolution and effects of a lightsaber combat. Other cards can affect your lightsaber combat total, Force loss from lightsaber combat, or even how you draw lightsaber combat destiny. A lightsaber is not required to initiate lightsaber combat (conceptually the lightsaber represents finely honed skill rather than the physical object).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "If a player cannot draw any destiny for lightsaber combat, treat their combat total as an unmodifiable 0. If one or both participants leave table before the winner and loser are determined, the combat immediately ends with no result.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Lightsaber Combat",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Combat Cards",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The Epic Event cards Inner Strength and Deep Hatred permit you to place combat cards under your Jedi (or Dark Jedi). The secards provide you with extra options for lightsaber combat or even duels, and represent the reserves of strength, combat training and use of the Force that a Jedi can call upon during combat. The cards you may place under your character must come from your hand.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Combat cards can be used to replace a single lightsaber combat or duel destiny draw. The destiny value of a combat card is a substituted destiny draw, but the physical card itself is not a destiny draw or just drawn. When a combat card is a substituted destiny, it can only be modified, canceled, or reset by other cards that specifically target combat cards.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Additional lightsaber combat or duel destiny draws may still be drawn normally (or substituted with another combat card, at the player's discretion).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Local Trouble Battle",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A Local Trouble battle is one where a battle has been initiated by a card titled Local Trouble. During a Local Trouble battle, any other characters, vehicles, or starships at the site that are not participating in the Local Trouble battle are inactive for the duration of the battle, with two specific exceptions:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• If a character participating in a Local Trouble battle is on an open vehicle, that vehicle is not inactive.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Cards that become inactive because of a Local Troubl ebattle are considered to be on table for the purposes of objectives.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Remember that these cards are inactive by rule, they are not considered excluded for any game-related purpose.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Mining Droid Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Timer Mines, Infantry Mines, and Vehicle Mines may be deployed by mining droids (even if Undercover) in two different ways, as indicated below (remember that because automated weapons only target cards present with them, characters enclosed on vehicles or starships are immune to them).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Appendix C",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Special Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Laying Mines",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "When your mining droid is present at any site, it may 'lay' (deploy face up) mines there (based upon the mine's game text).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Burying Mines",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "If your mining droid is present at an exterior planet site, it may 'bury' (place face down) mines there to simulate creation of a minefield. You may bury any number of cards from your hand face down underneath that site, but only during your deploy phase. You may choose to bury 'real' mines, 'duds' (non-mine cards buried to try and trick your opponent) or a mixture of the two. When any character, vehicle or starship deploys or moves to or across that site, all buried cards there are 'tripped' (revealed). Any duds are simply lost. Any mines 'explode,' targeting the card that tripped them if applicable. These mines target as normal automated weapons (see Automated Weapons rules). If buried mines are tripped during your turn and you have a mining droid present, you may choose to 'defuse' any or all of them (at normal use of the Force) before they explode.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Timer Mines",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The destiny draw for Timer Mines is not considered a weapon destiny (thus it can't be modified, substituted, etc., as if it were one). The phrase \"owner's choice\" on this automated weapon refers to the owner of the affected characters, not the owner of the Timer Mine.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Timer Mines do not affect your characters. If you trip your own buried Timer Mine, it is simply discarded.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Infantry And Vehicle Mines",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Infantry and Vehicle Mines are treated as \"all card\" situations (thus they may target inactive cards or your own cards). If two or more potential targets trigger one of your mines simultaneously, you may choose which one for the mine to target. See All Cards, Ch. 4.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Missing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Several cards cause characters to become disoriented or uncertain where they are, such as when Luke was in the wastes of Hoth after escaping the wampa's cave.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "This condition is defined in game terms as 'missing,' and is caused by cards such as Ice Storm, He Hasn't Come Back Yet, Always Thinking With Your Stomach, and Sandwhirl. Missing characte rscan be rescued using specific cards such as Found Someone You Have, or by forming a search party. A missing character is inactive.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 169,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Missing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Search Party",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "During your control phase, you may attempt to find missing characters (even if they went missing that same turn) by forming and using a search party as follows: 1) Designate one or more of your characters at the same site as the missing character(s) to be members of the search party. 2) Draw destiny. 3) Add 1 to the destiny draw for each member of the search party (2 if that search party character is a scout).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 170,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "4) If total destiny > 5, one of your missing characters there (random selection) is found and joins the search party.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 170,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "You may only search where you have one or more characters missing (you may not search for your opponent's characters). Members of a search party (including any characters they find) may not move, search again or participate in a battle you initiate for the remainder of that turn.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 170,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Podracing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The mechanics for a Podrace are contained on the Epic Event card Boonta Eve Podrace. Although you can par ticipate in a race without a Podracer card (the Epic Event permits you to place race destiny on the Podrace Arena), you'll find your chances of winning are greatly enhanced when using cards like Sebulba's Podracer or Anakin's Podracer.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 170,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "You can deploy more than one type of Podracer for a single race (although at this time, Light Side has only Anakin's Podracer available.) As specified on the Epic Event, you are only normally entitled to draw one race destiny per Control Phase, so if you have more than one Podracer at the Arena, you must state which Podracer you are drawing for before you draw the destiny.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 170,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Podracing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Race Destiny And Race Total",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
