            {
              "text": "When a game of Sabacc has already been initiated and a player is unable to draw the 2 cards necessary to begin the hand, the Sabacc game will i mmediately end with no result (see Empty Deck Or Pile) and any Sabacc cards already drawn are placed in their owner's Used Pile.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Senate",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The Galactic Senate was the center of government in the Old Republic, where conflicts were resolved with words rather than weapons. Battles may still take place there, but as is indicated by the site itself, during battles there a character's politics is treated as that character's power (for anything that checks power). Any card which modifies or resets a character's power at the Galactic Senate is ignored during battle (though you may still modify total power as normal).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Agenda",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A character's agenda(s) is listed in a sentence of their game text, immediately following the keyword \"Agenda(s).\" For example, Queen Amidala, Ruler Of Naboo has the agendas of 'justice' and 'peace.' Other game text can then provide a benefit or disadvantage based on a condition relating to that agenda. When either of them has one agenda that the other character also possesses.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Politics",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Some characters have a numerical value entitled Politics, whether it is printed (such as on Aks Moe) or provided by another card (such as the Objective My Lord, Is That Legal). This indicates their relative poli tical power when 'voting' (battling) at the Galactic Senate.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Politics functions differently than the normal rules of values. A character whose politics is 0 (even if temporarily reduced) is a character without politics.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "can have his politics added to by other game actions (though if a value is reset the normal reset rules will still apply). Whenever a player has no characters with politics at the Galactic Senate, their total politics is considered an unmodifiable zero.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Senate Majority",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A player has a \"senate majority\" when his or her total politics at the Galactic Senate is greater than the opponent's total politics there (in the case of a tie, characters at the Galactic Senate are then \"in a senate majority\".",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Stealing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "A card is stolen when one player is able to take the card from the other player and use it as his own (from that point out, the new player is considered the \"owner\").",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 172,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "The opponent may not take ownership of one of your cards unless a card or rule allows it to be stolen (or 'won' in the case of sabacc). If an action would result in",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "you placing an opponent's card into your own hand, Lost Pile or Life Force without actually 'stealing' the card, that action simply fails.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "For example, an empty landed starfighter cannot be allowing it to steal (or is targeted by another card wstolen simply by having an opponent's pilot walk up to it.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Stolen cards that are placed in the new owner's Life Force can be activated, used, lost, deployed, etc. as if they were still that player's cards (they are still considered ' stolen '). However, if a ' stolen ' card is stolen actually be able to use the weapon or devic back by its original owner, it is no longer considered a ' stolen ' card for any purposes. At the end of the game, stolen cards count towards their owner's Life Force total. This is different from a card your opponent inserts weapon that has been stolen may be targeted to be in your Reserve Deck, which is not considered stolen (and is still your opponent's card). See Insert Cards.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Stolen cards are returned to their rightful owners at the end of the game.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Note that any starship, vehicle, weapon, or device with the word 'Stolen' in title is always considered to be a stolen starship, vehicle, weapon, or device respectively, regardless of owner. Also, any card with 'Stolen' in title is still considered to be 'just stolen' whenever a player steals it.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Stealing Vehicles And Starships",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Even when a card allows stealing, an opponent's starship or vehicle may not be stolen if the opponent has characters aboard (except characters at a starship or vehicle site-those are permitted).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Note that permanent pilots/nav computers are not characters and thus do not prevent stealing.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "When you steal a vehicle or starship, any vehicles and starships in its cargo bay are simultaneously stolen as well, along with any weapons and devices deployed upon on any of these stolen cards or in their cargo Obi -Wan's Lightsaber is a character weapon, therefore bays. Each of these additional stolen cards simply remains on (or in) its respective vehicle or starship.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Other cards (such as Effects) also remain in place, but are not considered stolen.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "If the vehicle or starship has a permanent pilot, it is conceptu ally replaced by a permanent pilot of the opposite side of the Force possessing the same ability number and listed abilities. Any unique personas identified as permanent pilot/astromech are no longer those unique personas while the opposing player controls them. If returned to the original owner, it is restored to its original game text. If this would cause two versions of the unique persona to be on table, place one of them (and all cards on them) in owner's Used Pile.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Stealing Weapons And Devices",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The onl ytypes of weapons that may be stolen are character weapons [unless explicitly stated within a card's game text, 'won' as 'stakes' (See Sabacc), or being carried by a vehicle or starship which is itself stolen]. Only devices that may deploy upon (or be car ried by) a character may be stolen [again, unless explicitly stated within a card's game text, 'won' via Sabacc, or being carried by a vehicle or starship which is itself stolen]. Additionally, if a weapon or device is stolen while another device is deploy edupon it (e.g. a n Imperial Blaster is stolen while it has a Blaster Scope",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Appendix C",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Special Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "on it) then that additional device remains attached and is also stolen.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "For further examples, a character which has game text hich allows theft) may steal a lightsaber or a Light Repeating Blaster Cannon, but may not steal Proton Torpedoes, a Laser Gate, a Hydroponics Station or the Planet Defender Ion Cannon. The thief does not have to e, just carry it.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Remember that when a character is carrying a weapon or device they cannot use, that card is inactive. A stolen back (even if inactive). A stolen weapon or device follows the normal rules oftransfer (even if inactive) (See weapons-transferring).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Be aware that a character being a \"thief\" does not in itself give the ability to steal other cards. Additionally, even when a card allows stealing, a device may not be stolen if the opponent has characters being carried by that device.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Stealing",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Examples",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Reegesk steals Dash In Rogue 12. The Dash persona is removed from the card and replaced with a generic dark side permanent pilot who provides ability of 3.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Light is not prevented from deploying Dash Rendar, since he's no longer on board Rogue 12.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Reegesk attempts to steal a weapon from Light's Lost Pile: X -Wing Laser Cannon, Obi -Wan's Lightsaber, Medium Repeating Blaster Cannon are in the pile. Both the Medium Repeating Blaster Cannon and Obi -Wan 's Lightsaber can be carried by characters, but only the that is the only valid choice.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Trench Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The Death Star: Trench is an exterior mobile site with some special properties. It must deploy next to the Death Star system (it cannot deploy if the system is not on the table) as shown below.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Players may not deploy or move any cards to the Trench unless a specific card allows them to do so (e.g., Attack Run, Maneuver Check and the Death Star:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Trench itself).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Undercover Spy Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Certain cards can make your character into an \"undercover spy\", such as the Effect card Undercover or the Immediate Effect A Gift. When your spy goes undercover, place it on your opponent's side of the site (even if spywas on an enclosed vehicle).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Your undercover spy is considered inactive. However, the following exceptions apply:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 173,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Their game text remains active.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• You may still deploy Effects, weapons, and devices onto your undercover spy (they are not inactive).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Excep tduring battle, they may still be targeted by Interrupts, as well as cards that target spies (or ISB Agents), and anything that would cause them to be dueled, hit, or lost.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Undercover spies may still be attacked by creatures, and any creature attached to an undercover spy will remain active.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Note that these exceptions apply only towards the spy's state as an undercover spy, anything else (such as going missing or 'all cards' situations) overrides these exceptions. Also, when an undercover spy goes missing, the character remains undercover, but missing rules will override the undercover spy rules until they are found (thus while missing, they cannot use their game text, cannot have Effects, weapons, or devices deployed on them, they no longer prevent force drains from being initiated at their location, etc.).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Wherever you have an undercover spy:",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• Your opponent cannot Force drain.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "• You may deploy without presence or Force icons.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Undercover Spy Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Deploy as Undercover Spy",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Some characters may (or must) deploy as an undercover spy. In such cases, the character can only deploy to a site location, never aboard a vehicle or starship card. The character deploys to the opponent side of that location. Canceling the game text of such characters will not stop them fro mbeing undercover.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Deploying a card as an undercover spy still counts as deploying a character, a card with ability (if applicable), a card of that card type, a copy of that persona, etc. before going undercover.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Undercover Spy Rules",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "-",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Movement",
              "bold": true,
              "italic": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Undercove rspies may move using landspeed or docking bay transit, but these moves are performed during the opponent's move phase instead of their own.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "They still use the docking bay text on their owner's side of the location.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Undercover spies may move using the mov ement text on their owner's side of a location. Follow the instructions listed in the movement text. If the movement text does not specify a timeframe, the movement occurs during the opponent's move phase instead of their own.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Undercover spies may use land speed to move as a 'react', if permitted by their game text or a card that may target them, such as an Interrupt (e.g. Dash).",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Undercover spies may be relocated by cards that can target them, such as Interrupts. Remember who the owner of the undercover spy is. For example, Light's undercover spy could not \"tag along\" when Dark plays Elis Helrot, which relocates only Dark's cards. Follow the instructions and timing (if any) listed on the relocation card.",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Undercover spies may not move onto starship or vehicle cards, but they may move to starship sites or vehicle sites. If moving between a starship/vehicle site and the location the related starship/vehicle is present at, this occurs during the opponent's move phase, and",
              "bold": false,
              "italic": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "pageRef": 174,
          "textRuns": [
            {
              "text": "Appendix C",
